                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "comment": "",
                                    "id": "obj-9",
                                    "index": 1,
                                    "maxclass": "inlet",
                                    "numinlets": 0,
                                    "numoutlets": 1,
                                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                                    "patching_rect": [ 68.0, 1.0, 30.0, 30.0 ]
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
                                    "fontsize": 15.0,
                                    "id": "obj-4",
                                    "linecount": 20,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 11.0, 85.0, 989.0, 367.0 ],
                                    "presentation": 1,
                                    "presentation_linecount": 20,
                                    "presentation_rect": [ 8.0, 53.0, 970.0, 367.0 ],
                                    "text": "- First of all, connect the T-Stick and the computer to the same WiFi network. To setup a T-Stick, please refer to the \"Connect Guide\" in the T-Stick tab. Press the \"Enable\" button in the T-Stick tab and make sure that the patch is receiving data (the graphs below should start to display some data).\n- Go to the \"Routing\" tab and setup the Audio matrix, by connecting the modules you want to use to the effects and/or main output. At this point, you should begin hearing sound coming out (make sure that Max's audio is turned on). If you don't, go back to the \"Main\" section and make sure the volume of the instruments is turned on. If you don't hear the \"Scylla\" modules, make sure that they have received a MIDI note (press \"Advance\" on the sequencer module).\n- Go to the \"Mapping\" section and setup a dynamic modulation, by setting the LFOs and connecting them to the sound parameters. Make sure that before you start making connections, you have selected which neural networks they will be linked to using the \"Sound Gesture\" selector. If you don't hear the modulation being applied, make sure that the \"Modulation\" button is turned on.\n- When you are satisfied with the dynamic modulation, you can record a gesture to associate with it. First, select which motion features will be used in the appropriate Neural Network module (whose index corresponds to the one in the \"Sound Gesture matrix selector). When you are ready, press the \"Record\" button and start performing the gesture alongside the modulation. 3-5s should be ideal, but feel free to experiment. If you see an error in the Max console, clear the neural network and try again.\n- Train the model by pressing the \"Train NN\" button. When you see the error is low enough and is plateauing, you can turn training off. \n- Press the \"Predict\" button and perform the gesture. You should hear the sound parameters change while you are performing. \n- Repeat and have fun!\n\nNB: The preset section also stores the neural network models, so switching preset also changes the model that is being used to perform the gesture-sound mapping.",
                                    "textcolor": [ 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ]
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
                                    "fontsize": 24.0,
                                    "id": "obj-2",
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
