                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
                                    "fontsize": 15.0,
                                    "id": "obj-4",
                                    "linecount": 27,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 11.0, 85.0, 989.0, 493.0 ],
                                    "presentation": 1,
                                    "presentation_linecount": 28,
                                    "presentation_rect": [ 8.0, 53.0, 970.0, 511.0 ],
                                    "text": "The T-Stick Playground is a Max-based performance environment for the T-Stick, developed by modulo21 (Andrea Strata) during his IDMIL internship in Spring 2026. It uses a \"mapping by demonstration\" approach to create interesting gesture-to-sound mappings based on user examples.\n\nThe environment consists of a few different modules, all of which are available to be independently controlled with the T-Stick:\n- Two \"Scylla\" oscillator modules, controlled by a pitch sequencer. These two instruments can be modulated in frequency and phase by any signal in the environment (see the \"Routing\" tab).\n- One CCPM (Cross-Coupled Phase Modulation) module, which allows to generate a huge space of glitch-like timbres.\n- Two corpus-based sampling modules: one sample player (CE-Player) and one granular player (CE-Grain). These two modules can play samples by exploring a timbric space, provided by the CE-Matcher module and created through the \"Corpus Creator\" patch. The exploration of this 3D timbre space is connected by default to the Pitch, Yaw and Roll of the T-Stick, and returns the N samples that are closest to the current position (depending on the Num Neighbours parameter). For more information on these modules and on how to create your own corpora, please refer to the \"Corpus Creator\" patch.\n- Two stereo filters, with independent parameters for each channel.\n- A few audio effects, including a reverb, a distortion and a spectral delay.\n\nThe modules are routed to the Main output or to the effects using the \"Routing\" tab. The routing is dynamic and can be controlled with a neural network (see the \"Routing\" tab for more infos).\n\nThe T-Stick tab is used to connect to a T-Stick device, and to fine-tune the extracted motion features. Moreover, two motion triggers can be learned in this tab (see the \"T-Stick\" tab for more infos).\n\nThe \"Mapping\" tab is where the \"mapping by demonstration\" part of this environment happens. It is used to map motion features to the different modules' parameters using neural networks. In this section, there are three stereo LFOs, three Neural Network modules and a few routing matrices to connect the LFOs to the parameters. The core idea is to create a time-varying, modulated signal by connecting the LFOs to the synthesis parameters. These parameter curves can be sent to one of the neural networks, and the user can  perform a dynamic gesture while hearing the modulation to create a mapping (by training the model after the gesture is recorded). See the \"Mapping\" section for more infos on how to do this. ",
                                    "textcolor": [ 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ]
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "fontface": 1,
                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
