                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "comment": "",
                                                    "id": "obj-9",
                                                    "index": 1,
                                                    "maxclass": "inlet",
                                                    "numinlets": 0,
                                                    "numoutlets": 1,
                                                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                                                    "patching_rect": [ 68.0, 1.0, 30.0, 30.0 ]
                                                }
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "fontface": 1,
                                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
                                                    "fontsize": 15.0,
                                                    "id": "obj-4",
                                                    "linecount": 15,
                                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                                    "patching_rect": [ 11.0, 85.0, 983.0, 277.0 ],
                                                    "presentation": 1,
                                                    "presentation_linecount": 15,
                                                    "presentation_rect": [ 8.0, 53.0, 968.0, 277.0 ],
                                                    "text": "A neural network can be used to morph between an arbitrary number of matrix states using T-Stick features. This neural network is basically performing interpolation, contrary to the ones used in the mapping section (where a parameter envelope is associated with a dynamic gesture).\nFor this reason, the input features associated with each matrix state should be a static T-Stick configuration (like a specific brush position, FSR pressure level or device orientation). A few movements around the center value can be useful to prevent overfitting and steer the model in the current direction; you should however avoid big movements while recording a gesture for a single state.\n\nFirst you need to set all of the matrix states to morph between (see the Routing Matrix reference). With any added state, the \"map state n\" tab will update by displaying all the saved state IDs.\nOnce all desired states are saved, choose the T-Stick features to use as input to the neural network. In the \"map state n\" tab, set the state you're going to record an example for; the matrix will recall that state so you can hear it while you perform. Toggle \"record gesture\" while the T-Stick is in the orientation you wish to associate with the current state. Repeat this procedure for all the states you saved.\nAt this point, the model is ready to be trained and make predictions.\n\nAfter the model is trained, individual connections predicted by the neural network can be disabled and re-enabled using the connection editor (see the reference for more infos). ",
                                                    "textcolor": [ 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ]
                                                }
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "fontface": 1,
                                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
