                                                }
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "comment": "",
                                                    "id": "obj-9",
                                                    "index": 1,
                                                    "maxclass": "inlet",
                                                    "numinlets": 0,
                                                    "numoutlets": 1,
                                                    "outlettype": [ "" ],
                                                    "patching_rect": [ 68.0, 1.0, 30.0, 30.0 ]
                                                }
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "fontface": 1,
                                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
                                                    "fontsize": 15.0,
                                                    "id": "obj-4",
                                                    "linecount": 27,
                                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                                    "patching_rect": [ 11.0, 85.0, 653.0, 493.0 ],
                                                    "presentation": 1,
                                                    "presentation_linecount": 27,
                                                    "presentation_rect": [ 8.0, 53.0, 654.0, 493.0 ],
                                                    "text": "A trigger gesture is used to initiate a specific event, based on wether a certain threshold is surpassed by one or more motion features. These thresholds can also be learned through demonstration, with the same high level approach used throughout this project.\n\nThe user first needs to press the \"Record\" button, and then perform the intended trigger gesture. The algorithm will detect the most prominent motion features involved in performing that gesture, and extract thresholds for them. The detected features and thresholds will be displayed after the gesture is recorded. The available motion features for this are angular velocity (all directions), FSR, brush speed. Note that all of the learned thresholds (excluding the FSR) include direction: angular acceleration towards the left is not the same as towards the right.\nAlso note that when \"record\" is pressed, the actual gesture is recorded from when the global activation threshold is surpassed (to avoid noise in the data). See the \"Features\" reference for more infos.\n\nAfter recording, the \"selectivity\" and \"sensitivity\" parameters allow to fine tune the recorded gesture. Selectivity controls the minimum value for a feature to be considered in the gesture. The higher the value, the more selective the algorithm will be, using only the most prominent features. The \"sensitivity\" parameter tunes the threshold for the selected features: the higher the value, the lower the threshold, so that it's easier to repeat the gesture.\n\nAfter a trigger gesture is learned, repeating it (surpassing the threshold for all features at the same time) will cause the trigger to fire. The two triggers are mapped to two events connected to the instruments: trigger 1 advances the sequencer that controls the two Scylla modules, while trigger 2 plays the sound sample currently selected by the CE-Player module.\nEach trigger also outputs a MIDI-style velocity, proportional to the interval between the crossing of the low and high threshold for all the features involved.",
                                                    "textcolor": [ 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 ]
                                                }
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "box": {
                                                    "fontface": 1,
                                                    "fontname": "Ableton Sans Medium",
                                                    "fontsize": 24.0,
